title: "Free Tool to Check a Redacted PDF for Leaks"
description: "Test a redacted PDF for text under black bars, hidden text, redactions that were not applied and metadata. Runs in your browser. Nothing is uploaded."
faq:
  - q: "Is the Redaction Checker free?"
    a: "Yes. The tool is free, with no account, no sign-up and no limit on how often you use it."
  - q: "Is my PDF uploaded to a server?"
    a: "No. The page has no server code and uses no AI model. PDF.js reads the PDF and draws the pages in your browser, and your file never leaves your device."
  - q: "How do I check if a PDF is properly redacted?"
    a: "Open the PDF in the Redaction Checker. It looks for text that is still in the file under black bars or white boxes, redactions that were marked but not applied, earlier versions saved in the file, comments, form values, attached files and metadata. Each leak shows in a list and is marked on the page."
  - q: "Can the text under a black box in a PDF be recovered?"
    a: "Often, yes. If the box was only drawn on top of the page, the text is still in the file. Anyone can select and copy it, or open the file in another app. The Redaction Checker shows you this text so you can fix the file before you share it."
  - q: "Is the check 100% accurate?"
    a: "No. The checker reads the text layer of the PDF. It does not read text inside images, so it cannot tell you what a scanned page shows. A clean result means the checks found no leaked text, not that the PDF is safe in every way. Always look at the pages yourself too."
  - q: "Does the Redaction Checker change my PDF?"
    a: "No. It only reads the file. To fix a leak, use the PDF Redactor, which makes a new PDF with the text removed under each bar."
  - q: "Can I save the results?"
    a: "Yes. Click \"Download report\" to save the findings as a .txt file, with the page number of each item."
  - q: "Can I check a PDF that has a password?"
    a: "No. Remove the password in your PDF app first, then open the PDF in the checker."
